---
/**
 * foodcraft / CredentialsBO — "Sipsip Tea Bar"'s standing on record.
 *
 * ROLE. The design's CREDENTIALS band: ONE white plate at radius 32px, padded
 * 44px 42px, floating on the page's own blush ground. Inside it a two-column
 * split — a fixed-max 300px head column holding the lilac eyebrow pill and the
 * display headline, then 44px of gap, then a two-up grid of credential cells at
 * a 16px gutter. Each cell is a pale blush box at radius 20px, padded 20px
 * 22px, opening with a 34px solid-purple round badge carrying a numeral, then
 * an 18px Baloo 2 title, then a 13.5px sentence. The two columns are centred on
 * each other, as drawn. Nothing here is sharp and nothing here is ruled: this
 * band is drawn entirely with radii and grounds.
 *
 * It is the band a reader is most likely to take as fact — a permit, a
 * certification, a log — so not one word of it is written by this component.
 *
 * DESIGN FIDELITY. Every value below is lifted from the CREDENTIALS element of
 * "Sipsip Tea Bar Landing.dc.html" and mapped hex -> token (R1). There is not
 * one literal hex in this file.
 *   plate        #FFFFFF ..................................... var(--card)
 *                radius 32px · padding 44px 42px (lengths, verbatim)
 *   split        minmax(0,300px) minmax(0,1fr) · gap 44px · align-items center
 *   eyebrow      11px / 700 / .14em / uppercase, #5C3580 on a #EEE2F5 pill,
 *                padding 8px 16px, radius 100px
 *                ...... var(--accent-deep) on var(--lilac), var(--r-pill).
 *                The wrapper's .kick already carries 11px/700/uppercase/
 *                inline-block AND the shared 520 size step, so only the pill
 *                (ground, label, padding, corner) and the design's .14em
 *                tracking are restated. AboutBO restates exactly this set.
 *   headline     Baloo 2 700 · clamp(26px,3.2vw,40px) / 1.1 · margin .24em 0 0
 *                · #241A22 ..... var(--ink) via the wrapper's .h2; the purple
 *                emphasis span (#7B4FA8) is var(--accent) via PageBO's global
 *                `:where(.h2 em)`. Size, leading and margin ARE restated — see
 *                DECISION 2, this headline is smaller than the shared .h2.
 *   cells grid   repeat(2,minmax(0,1fr)) · gap 16px ...... verbatim, var-free
 *   cell         #FDF7FA · radius 20px · padding 20px 22px
 *                ................. see DECISION 1 — the one colour in this
 *                element that no PageBO token holds exactly.
 *   badge        34x34 · radius 50% · #7B4FA8 ground / #FFF7F2 label · Baloo 2
 *                700 16px · centred · margin-bottom 11px
 *                ...... var(--accent) / var(--on-accent) / var(--r-pill), and
 *                the face, weight and figures come from the wrapper's .fig.
 *   title        Baloo 2 600 · 18px · margin 0 0 6px · #241A22, and NO
 *                line-height and NO letter-spacing declared — both compute to
 *                the initial value in the design document ..... var(--ink);
 *                see DECISION 4 for why both are restated here.
 *   body         13.5px / 1.7 · #5D5158 · margin 0 ......... var(--ink-soft)
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS — all four are declared in components/editor/
 * genericContentSchema.ts under section id 'credentials'. Nothing below is a
 * dead hook, and no path outside this list is written:
 *   credentials.tag ................ the eyebrow pill
 *   credentials.headline ........... the h2, fed with set:html so an owner can
 *                                    place the design's purple emphasis
 *                                    anywhere inside their OWN sentence
 *   credentials.items.${i}.title ... the 18px cell title
 *   credentials.items.${i}.body .... the sentence under it. CANONICAL key is
 *                                    `body` — that is what the schema declares
 *                                    ({ title: '', body: '' }) and what the
 *                                    sidebar writes. The reader below also
 *                                    ACCEPTS desc / detail / description as
 *                                    INPUT, because astro-builder's
 *                                    normalizeBlock copies body into desc and
 *                                    older records were saved that way, but the
 *                                    hook it emits always spells `body`.
 *
 * DERIVED — computed here, therefore carrying NO data-field:
 *   · the badge numeral. It is built from the DISPLAY index and zero-padded to
 *     two digits, reproducing the design's own '01'…'04' seed, so the list
 *     always counts without gaps however many blank rows were dropped and an
 *     edit can never be misdirected into a counter (R6). aria-hidden: it is
 *     ornament, and the title beside it is the content (R9).
 *   · the cells' track count — two at two rows and above, exactly one at a
 *     single row, so one lone credential fills its column instead of hanging
 *     half-width off its left edge.
 *   · the split's track count — one track when there is no head to hold, so the
 *     cells never sit in the 300px column with the rest of the plate blank
 *     beside them.
 *   · the reveal stagger, from the DISPLAY position, capped at the wrapper's
 *     three declared delay steps.
 *
 * R2 — NOTHING IS SUPPLIED BY THIS FILE. content.credentials has no derived
 * fallback at all: astro-builder passes `c.credentials ?? undefined` and
 * lib/derive-content-defaults.ts never invents a credential (its own header
 * names credentials as a thing it must not fabricate). Consequences, all
 * deliberate:
 *   · with no filled row the WHOLE <section> is omitted — no plate, no eyebrow,
 *     no headline, no padding (R5: the gate counts the ROWS and never the tag
 *     or the headline). The mechanism is NOT keepHead — that helper is handed
 *     `(derived as any).credentials`, which derive-content-defaults does not
 *     emit, so it returns the block untouched here; it is normalizeBlock, whose
 *     "keep the wrapper" branch hands back `{ tag, headline, items: [] }` for a
 *     block that carries a head and no list. A head whose every row was cleared
 *     therefore arrives intact, and a gate counting tag or headline would ship a
 *     white plate holding a heading over nothing.
 *   · title and body are guarded independently and OUTSIDE their tags, so a
 *     half-filled row prints only what was typed — never an empty <h3> holding
 *     open 6px of margin under the badge, and never a cell box drawn around
 *     nothing.
 *   · those guards are trim-AWARE only because the reader coerces and trims
 *     once, up front: a title of "   " is truthy, and guarded raw it would ride
 *     through the row filter on its sibling's body and print exactly that empty
 *     heading. Trimming is not supplying content — the owner's words are
 *     untouched, only the whitespace around them.
 *   · the head guard sits outside its column div too, so an owner who typed
 *     neither field gets no empty box beside the list.
 *   · the design's demo strings — "Credentials", "Clean bar, every shift.",
 *     "Sanitary permit", "Food safety trained", "Daily bar log", "Sealed to go"
 *     — are the DESIGNER filling in their own template. A bar that holds no
 *     city permit must not have this page claim one, and staff who have not sat
 *     a food-handling course must not be captioned as certified.
 *
 * DELIBERATELY NOT RENDERED, so none of it reads as an oversight:
 *   · NO star row. R2's rating rule is real but has nothing to bind to here: a
 *     credential carries no numeric rating field in the schema, so five drawn
 *     stars would be a fabricated claim about a permit. This design draws its
 *     stars in TESTIMONIALS, not in CREDENTIALS.
 *   · NO seal, shield, ribbon, stamp, tick or badge-of-office of any kind, and
 *     no logo strip. A drawn certification mark is a visual claim the owner
 *     never made; the design draws none either. The purple numeral disc is a
 *     counter, not an emblem, and it is aria-hidden precisely so nothing reads
 *     it as one.
 *   · NO .plate, NO .ph, NO role="img", NO .cap. (The white box below is
 *     .sip-cred-panel, deliberately NOT named for the wrapper's .plate — that
 *     primitive is an image slot with a .ph wash and a .cap pill, and none of
 *     it belongs here.) The design gives this band no
 *     photograph, so there is no image slot to offer and no accessible name to
 *     invent for one (R9).
 *   · NO button and NO link of any kind — the design gives CREDENTIALS neither.
 *     So none of the forbidden theme-engine class names (.btn-primary
 *     .btn-ghost .nav-cta .cta-band .trust .testi) appear here and `.hero` is
 *     not on this <section> (R4). Nothing in this band is focusable, so the
 *     wrapper's focus ring never has to resolve inside it.
 *   · NO onPage() anchor-remap helper, and NO id. This is a decision, not an
 *     omission: 'credentials' is not one of PageBO's ids (menu about gallery
 *     visit faq main), nothing on the page links here, and — decisively — this
 *     section emits no href at all, so PAGE_ANCHORS / ANCHOR_ALIAS would be an
 *     unused const with nothing to remap, which is what a reviewer would flag
 *     instead. R7 exists to stop an anchor pointing at an id this page has not
 *     got, not to require a helper where there is no anchor. TrustBO,
 *     CredentialsBM and CredentialsBL each recorded the same call. If a later
 *     revision adds an action to this band, copy the helper from HeaderBO
 *     wholesale rather than hand-rolling a shorter one, with PAGE_ANCHORS =
 *     menu about gallery visit faq main and the family's ANCHOR_ALIAS
 *     (location|contact -> visit, services|rooms -> menu, host|why -> about,
 *     work -> gallery, book -> main); off-page hrefs (tel: mailto: http) pass
 *     through untouched.
 *   · NO band-pink, NO band-sage, NO band-lilac. The wrapper offers all three
 *     and this section opts into none, because the design keeps CREDENTIALS on
 *     the page's own blush ground with a white plate on top of it. Banding it
 *     would put a pastel behind a pastel-washed cell and flatten the two-step
 *     ground the whole element is drawn on. The lilac and the purple appear
 *     here only where the design puts them: inside the eyebrow pill and inside
 *     the numeral disc.
 *   · NO data-block marker. editorBridge only needs one where two hook prefixes
 *     share a <section>; everything here is credentials.*, so the Blocks toggle
 *     already resolves by hook weight.
 *
 * DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE QUESTION:
 *
 *   1. THE CELL WASH IS --paper, AND IT IS THE ONE APPROXIMATION IN THIS FILE.
 *      FLAGGED FOR REVIEW. The design fills each credential cell with #FDF7FA,
 *      a very pale pink-white. No PageBO token holds that value: the nearest
 *      declared colours are --paper and --tint, which are BOTH #FFF7F2 — the
 *      same lightness, a shade warmer rather than pinker — while --pink-2
 *      (#F7DCE7) and --lilac (#EEE2F5) are far more saturated and are not it.
 *      R1 forbids inventing the hex, so the choice is between the two tokens
 *      that share a value, and it is --paper for a reason that only shows under
 *      an admin scheme: buildOverrideCss owns --paper, --card, --ink and
 *      --ink-soft and repaints all four together, so a --paper cell keeps
 *      exactly the design's relationship to its --card plate AND keeps the
 *      title and body text on the ground the engine pairs them with. --tint is
 *      invisible to that file and would stay a bright cream under a repainted
 *      palette, stranding relit text on it. On the native palette the two are
 *      indistinguishable and the drawing is the design's.
 *
 *   2. THE HEADLINE SCALE IS RESTATED, AND THE WRAPPER'S 760 PASS IS
 *      DELIBERATELY SUPERSEDED. This band's h2 is the design's SMALL headline —
 *      clamp(26px,3.2vw,40px)/1.1 — where the wrapper's shared .h2 is
 *      clamp(28px,3.9vw,52px)/1.12. The local rule compiles to two classes plus
 *      Astro's scope attribute — (0,3,0) — and so beats the wrapper's
 *      `html[data-page="BO"] :where(.h2)`, which is (0,1,1), at every width, INCLUDING inside its `@media (max-width:760px)` block, and
 *      that is required rather than incidental: the wrapper's mobile pass
 *      resolves to 38px just under 760px while this band's own clamp is already
 *      sitting on its 26px floor by 761px, so letting the shared rule win would
 *      make the headline JUMP FROM 26px TO 38px as the viewport NARROWS past
 *      760 — the grow-as-it-shrinks bug two audits have already caught in this
 *      family. The design's clamp is monotonic across every breakpoint here
 *      (40px at 1250 and up · 28.8px at 900 · 26px at 812 and below, since
 *      3.2vw meets the 26px floor at 812.5px) and is therefore never restated
 *      in a media query below.
 *
 *   3. THE WRAPPER'S 22ch MEASURE CAP IS LEFT IN PLACE, not released. The
 *      design's CREDENTIALS h2 carries no max-width and does not need one: its
 *      column is capped at 300px, narrower than 22ch of 40px Baloo 2, so the
 *      cap never bites at desktop and the design draws exactly as authored.
 *      Between 900px (where the split collapses and the headline goes full
 *      width) and 760px (where the wrapper releases the cap itself) it is the
 *      only thing holding a sane measure, so removing it would be a regression
 *      with no fidelity gain. CredentialsBM records the same call.
 *
 *   4. BOTH RESTATED VALUES ON THE TITLE ARE THE DESIGN'S OWN INITIAL VALUES,
 *      not chosen numbers. The design's h3 declares neither line-height nor
 *      letter-spacing, so in that document both compute to the initial value.
 *      This wrapper is not so kind: its `:where(h1,h2,h3,h4)` reset pins
 *      line-height 1.12 and letter-spacing -.01em on every heading, which on an
 *      18px two-line title would set it tighter than the design ever draws it
 *      and would negatively track a face whose whole character is its rounded
 *      terminals. Writing `line-height: normal` and `letter-spacing: normal`
 *      reproduces the drawing exactly and needs no judgement call. Nothing else
 *      about the title is guessed: face (inherited --disp), weight, size,
 *      colour and margin are all the design's.
 *
 *   5. THE BADGE REUSES .fig AND OVERRIDES ITS COLOUR, WHICH IS NOT OPTIONAL.
 *      .fig already gives var(--disp) at 700 with tabular lining figures and
 *      line-height 1 — four of the design's values for this numeral, verbatim.
 *      Three things are local: the 16px, the disc, and the colour. The colour
 *      MUST be local, because .fig is var(--accent) and this badge's ground is
 *      var(--accent): left alone the numeral would be purple on purple. It is
 *      var(--on-accent), which is the on-colour the wrapper declares for that
 *      fill.
 *
 *   6. THE BADGE IS min-width/min-height 34px WITH 3px OF SIDE PADDING, not a
 *      hard 34x34 box. For every numeral this file can produce up to '99' the
 *      minimums govern and the badge renders at exactly 34x34 as specified; a
 *      list long enough to reach three digits then grows the disc into a pill
 *      rather than spilling its figure over the edge. border-radius is
 *      var(--r-pill) and not the design's literal `50%`: on a 34px box these
 *      are the SAME drawing, computed and not assumed — the two 100px radii on
 *      a side sum to 200 > 34, so CSS scales both by 34/200, giving 17px, which
 *      is exactly half of 34 and therefore a full circle. On a grown pill the
 *      same scaling gives fully rounded ends, which keeps the badge on this
 *      family's pill vocabulary in both states.
 *
 *   7. THE PLATE AND CELL RADII ARE LITERAL LENGTHS (32px and 20px), and that
 *      does not breach R1, which governs COLOUR. --r-lg is 26px and --r-md is
 *      18px, so no token holds either number, and rounding to the nearest would
 *      be a different drawing on the two shapes this whole section is made of.
 *      TrustBO keeps the design's 22px card radius for the same reason.
 *
 *   8. SECTION PADDING IS THE WRAPPER'S, not this file's. The design writes
 *      `padding:60px 28px 0`: the 1200/gutter half is the shared .wrap, and the
 *      top is `html[data-page="BO"] section`'s clamp(48px,6.4vw,80px). That
 *      clamp runs 20px over the design's 60px at wide widths and meets it at
 *      937.5px, and the design's bottom-0 is a WHOLE-PAGE rhythm decision — every
 *      unbanded section in this document pads top only — which belongs in the
 *      wrapper's shared rule. One section re-cutting its own vertical rhythm
 *      while fourteen siblings keep theirs is exactly the local re-derivation
 *      that puts a template out of step with its design. AboutBO flagged the
 *      identical mismatch and left it to the wrapper; so does this file.
 *      FLAGGED, not done here.
 *
 *   9. THE PLATE'S TWO MOBILE PADDING STEPS ARE OURS, and they are the only
 *      spacing in this file that is not the design's. The design specifies no
 *      narrow layout at all, so the responsive pass is this section's to own
 *      (R8). Side padding is spent TWICE before any text — the plate's 42px and
 *      then the cell's own 22px, 64px a side — which UNSTEPPED would leave
 *      331 − 2×64 = 203px of the 331px .wrap (92vw of a 360px viewport) for a
 *      13.5px sentence. The plate steps to 38px/34px at 900 and 30px/24px at
 *      520, so what actually renders at 360 is 331 − 2×(24 + 22) = 239px inner;
 *      both steps are strictly smaller than the one above them, so the ladder
 *      is monotonic. The CELL's own 20px 22px is kept verbatim at every width:
 *      stacking does not change what it buys.
 *
 * R8 — THE CLAMP LADDER, traced across all four widths, because two audits in
 * this family found type that GREW as the viewport narrowed. This file declares
 * exactly ONE clamp, the headline's, and it is never restated in a media query,
 * so it cannot be outranked by a floor below a cap. Every other size here is
 * FLAT, and only the column counts and the plate padding change at 900 and 520.
 * At 360px nothing can push sideways: the eyebrow, the headline, the titles and
 * the bodies all carry overflow-wrap: anywhere, and every grid track is
 * minmax(0, …), so a pasted URL cannot widen the plate. There is no button in
 * this section, so the both-properties rule for a full-width label has nothing
 * to apply to here.
 *
 * R3 — one scoped style block, no is:global. The headline is injected with
 * set:html, so scoped rules cannot reach an <em> inside it; its purple, and the
 * font-style: normal that stops Baloo 2 being faked into an oblique, both come
 * from PageBO's global `:where(.h2 em)`. That is why no :global() rule appears
 * below — nothing else in this file is set:html. (No literal end-style-tag is
 * written inside any comment here or in the stylesheet: the parser would end
 * the sheet on the spot and silently truncate the file.)
 *
 * CONTRAST — quoting the figures PageBO and TrustBO each computed from the
 * declared hexes (sRGB relative luminance, WCAG 2.x); none is estimated here:
 *   --ink on --card 16.86 ......... the headline, on the white plate
 *   --ink on --paper 15.92 ........ the 18px cell titles, on the cell wash
 *   --ink-soft on --paper 7.13 .... the 13.5px cell bodies
 *   --accent-deep on --lilac 7.40 . the eyebrow pill (recomputed here from
 *                                   the declared hexes: 7.3956; TrustBO's
 *                                   comment truncates the same figure to 7.39)
 *   --on-accent on --accent 5.63 .. the numeral in its disc (PageBO records
 *                                   this pair as "paper on an accent fill");
 *                                   aria-hidden in any case
 * The headline's <em> is --accent, which PageBO computes at 5.63 on --paper;
 * it sits here on --card instead, and that pair is strictly better rather than
 * estimated — --card is the lighter of the two grounds and --accent is the
 * darker member of both pairs, which is the same direction PageBO's own
 * ink/paper 15.92 against ink/card 16.86 shows.
 * CAVEAT, the same one PageBO makes for --muted: buildOverrideCss owns --paper,
 * --card, --ink, --ink-soft, --accent, --accent-deep and --on-accent and wins each with
 * !important, so every figure above is the NATIVE palette's and not a guarantee
 * across every admin scheme. --lilac is invisible to that file, so only the
 * eyebrow's ground holds fixed. The pairing this design can actually break —
 * --accent on --pink at 3.93 — does not occur anywhere in this section: there
 * is no pink ground here at all.
 *
 * Scoped class names are prefixed `sip-cred-` for THIS template, matching
 * AboutBO's `sip-about-` and TrustBO's `sip-proof-`. They are NOT a sibling's
 * names re-used: an audit had to rename a whole set that had been copied
 * verbatim between two files in this family.
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBO hands it to all fifteen sections
// uniformly. This band reads nothing outside content.*, so it is never
// destructured.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

// The schema declares 'credentials.items' with fallbackPaths ['credentials'],
// so the stored value is legitimately EITHER { tag, headline, items: [...] } or
// a bare array. Both shapes are read; only the schema's path is ever written.
const cred = content?.credentials || {};

// COERCE, THEN TRIM, ONCE AT THE READER. Two reasons, both real:
//   · a value that arrives as a NUMBER — an owner who typed `2024` into a
//     credential title, or an older AI save — has no .trim(), so calling it in
//     the row filter below would throw and take the whole build with it.
//   · trimming here is what makes every guard downstream trim-AWARE (see R2 in
//     the block above).
const str = (v: unknown): string =>
  typeof v === 'string' ? v : typeof v === 'number' && Number.isFinite(v) ? String(v) : '';

// The eyebrow is credentials.tag ONLY. The design's "Credentials" literal is
// the designer filling in their own template; it is not this file's to type.
const tag = str(cred.tag).trim();
const headline = str(cred.headline).trim();

type Item = {
  title?: unknown; label?: unknown;
  body?: unknown; desc?: unknown; detail?: unknown; description?: unknown;
};
const items: Item[] = Array.isArray(cred.items)
  ? cred.items
  : (Array.isArray(cred) ? cred : []);

const titleOf = (it: Item): string => (str(it.title).trim() || str(it.label).trim());
// Reads the aliases as INPUT (older saves, and normalizeBlock's desc copy) but
// the data-field emitted below always spells the canonical `body`. `||` rather
// than `??` deliberately: normalizeBlock copies body into desc whenever desc is
// merely absent, so a row saved with body:'' and a real desc must fall through
// rather than stop on the empty string.
const bodyOf = (it: Item): string =>
  (str(it.body).trim() || str(it.desc).trim() || str(it.detail).trim() || str(it.description).trim());

// R6 — a row an owner added but never filled would otherwise draw a blush box,
// a purple disc and 20px of padding over nothing. Drop the blanks (whitespace
// counts as blank) but carry the ORIGINAL index, so every data-field still
// writes to the real array slot: removing credential 2 must never redirect an
// edit of credential 3 into slot 2. The numeral uses the DISPLAY index instead,
// because it is decorative and must keep counting 01, 02, 03 without gaps.
const rows = items
  .map((it, i: number) => ({ title: titleOf(it), body: bodyOf(it), i }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.body));

const hasHead = Boolean(tag || headline);
// Two tracks is the design; one lone credential takes one track rather than
// hanging half-width off the left edge of its column.
const cellCols = Math.min(rows.length, 2);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="sip-cred">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="sip-cred-panel">
        <div class:list={['sip-cred-split', !hasHead && 'sip-cred-split--solo']}>
          {hasHead && (
            <div class="sip-cred-head reveal">
              {tag && <span class="kick sip-cred-kick" data-field="credentials.tag">{tag}</span>}
              {headline && (
                <h2 class="h2 sip-cred-h" data-field="credentials.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>
              )}
            </div>
          )}
          <div class:list={['sip-cred-cells', `sip-cred-cells--${cellCols}`]}>
            {rows.map((r, k: number) => (
              <div class="sip-cred-cell reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
                {/* DISPLAY index — the list reads 01, 02, 03 however many blank
                    rows were dropped. No data-field: it is a counter, never
                    stored. ONE literal aria-hidden and never a conditional one
                    beside it (R9). */}
                <span class="fig sip-cred-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
                {r.title && (
                  <h3 class="sip-cred-t" data-field={`credentials.items.${r.i}.title`}>{r.title}</h3>
                )}
                {r.body && (
                  <p class="sip-cred-p" data-field={`credentials.items.${r.i}.body`}>{r.body}</p>
                )}
              </div>
            ))}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* Colour, type and metrics come only from PageBO's token vocabulary
     (--card --paper --ink --ink-soft --accent --accent-deep --on-accent --lilac
     --disp --r-pill, plus the .wrap, .kick, .h2, .fig and .reveal primitives).
     There is not one literal hex in this file (R1); the literal numbers below
     are LENGTHS, which R1 does not govern.

     (Never write a literal end-style-tag inside a comment in here — the parser
      would end the stylesheet on the spot and silently truncate the file.) */

  /* The section keeps PageBO's shared vertical rhythm and the page's own blush
     ground; the white is on the PLATE, not on the band. See DECISION 8. */
  .sip-cred { min-width: 0; }

  /* THE PLATE, verbatim: white, radius 32px, padded 44px 42px. No token holds
     32px (--r-lg is 26px), and this is a length, so the design's number
     stands — DECISION 7. */
  .sip-cred-panel {
    min-width: 0;
    background-color: var(--card);
    border-radius: 32px;
    padding: 44px 42px;
  }

  /* THE SPLIT: a head column capped at the design's 300px against the rest of
     the plate, 44px apart, the two columns centred on each other as drawn. */
  .sip-cred-split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 300px) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 44px;
    align-items: center;
  }
  /* An owner who typed neither tag nor headline gets no head column, so the
     cells must not be left sitting in the 300px track. */
  .sip-cred-split--solo { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }

  .sip-cred-head { min-width: 0; }

  /* THE EYEBROW. The wrapper's .kick already carries this design's type — 11px,
     700, uppercase, inline-block — and owns the whole size curve (it steps to
     10.5px at 520), so no font-size is written here. What .kick does not know is
     that in THIS design the eyebrow is a PILL: a lilac ground, the deep purple
     label, 8px/16px of padding and the 100px corner. Those four and the design's
